---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import DBox from '../../components/DBox.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="A status light is a claim — Andrew Yang"
  description="Four lying status lights — a Synced badge that promised nothing, a cron green for eleven days after it died — and the principle that every green must be earned."
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/lab/`}>← Lab</a></p>
  <span class="tag">observability</span>
  <h1>A status light is a claim — make every green earned</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The most dangerous state in operations is not red; it is a green that
    nobody has to earn. After
    <a href={`${base}/lab/one-machine-one-operator/`}>hunting silent
    failures</a> for a while, a pattern emerged: most of them were hiding
    behind an indicator that was telling the truth about the wrong thing.
  </p>

  <Diagram
    viewBox="0 0 920 172"
    alt="An indicator showing green flows into the audit question: what would this look like if the claim were false? Two exits. If the answer is 'exactly the same' — the dashed path — the light is decoration, and the signal must be restructured: split timestamps, immutable tags, zero tolerated reds. If failure would look different, it is monitoring, and the green is earned."
    caption="The audit that caught four lying lights, as a flowchart. The dashed exit is where the eleven-day-green dead cron lived."
  >
    <DBox x={10} y={56} w={230} h={60} title="an indicator shows green" sub="every light is a claim" />
    <DBox x={335} y={56} w={250} h={60} accent title="ask for its alibi" sub={'"what would false look like?"'} />
    <DBox x={660} y={10} w={250} h={60} dashed title={'"exactly the same"'} sub="decoration — restructure the" sub2="signal until false looks different" />
    <DBox x={660} y={102} w={250} h={60} title={'"visibly different"'} sub="monitoring — this green" sub2="is earned" />
    <path class="flow" d="M240,86 L329,86" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow dash" d="M585,76 L620,76 L620,40 L654,40" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M585,96 L620,96 L620,132 L654,132" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
  </Diagram>

  <h2>Four lying lights, four fixes</h2>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>"Synced" that promised nothing:</strong> deployments pinned to a
      <code>:latest</code> image tag made the GitOps dashboard show Synced
      while saying nothing about <em>which code</em> was running. Fixed by
      pinning immutable per-commit tags, bumped by the pipeline — now Synced
      means "this exact build," and rollback is a git revert.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A cron that was green because it was dead:</strong> the firewall
      verification job exported "last run succeeded" — and kept exporting it
      for eleven days after the job stopped running. The metric now splits
      <em>last-ran</em> from <em>last-succeeded</em>, and staleness itself
      alerts. A timestamp that never gets old is the signature of a corpse.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A red that meant nothing:</strong> one unconfigured component was
      the only permanently-failing target on the board. Removing it was an
      observability fix, not a cleanup — a board with one tolerated red trains
      you to tolerate the second one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The pipeline nobody watched:</strong> the deployment system
      itself — the thing every other guarantee routes through — was the least
      instrumented component on the machine. It now has its own alerts, and the
      chain of "who watches the watcher" terminates off-machine in an external
      dead-man switch.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The principle</h2>
  <p>
    Every status indicator makes a claim, and the engineering question is
    always: <em>what would this look like if the claim were false?</em> If the
    answer is "exactly the same," the indicator is decoration. The fixes above
    are all the same move — restructuring the signal so that the failure case
    is visibly different from the healthy case: immutable tags, split
    timestamps, zero tolerated reds, watchers that are themselves watched.
  </p>

  <h2>Verification as a habit, not an event</h2>
  <p>
    The same standard applies to one-off claims. A cutover is "done" when a
    test matrix passes against the new path — not when the config is applied.
    A cold boot "works" when the machine has actually been rebooted and a
    scripted checklist has passed — twice, because the second drill is what
    proves the first one wasn't luck. An alert route "delivers" when a
    synthetic alert has arrived on a phone. Green you haven't earned is just
    red you haven't met yet.
  </p>

  <h2>What I'd tell my past self</h2>
  <p>
    Audit your dashboards by asking each light for its alibi: "when did you
    last prove you could turn red?" Anything that can't answer is not
    monitoring — it's reassurance. The eleven-day green cron taught me more
    about observability than any incident that actually paged.
  </p>
</Base>
